 "customerTurns": 8,
 "transcript": [
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "d/w not cleaning pls help, gritty bits left on glasses n bowls, beko"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "Before you start, switch it off and unplug it. This issue is usually caused by blocked spray arms or a dirty filter. You should check these components for debris and clean them thoroughly. From what you have described, the most likely cause is drain filter (clean first - gritty/sandy residue). Take out the lower rack, twist and lift out the filter in the bottom of the tub, and rinse it under a tap — a clogged filter starves the spray arms of water. Was the filter dirty or clogged (and is it clean now), or was it already clean?\n[Helpful to see: image \"Where the filter is\" — The filter sits in the base of the dishwasher, usually under the bottom spray arm. Twist it out anticlockwise and rinse it under a tap. Typical layout — your dishwasher may differ.]"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "ok just checked, filter was full of food n grease. cleaning it now"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "From what you have described, the most likely cause is a dirty filter. Now run a normal programme with a typical load. Are the dishes coming out clean now, or still dirty?"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "ok filter's rinsed n back in. i usually do the quick 30 min wash tho, is that ok or shud i do a normal one?"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "A normal cycle is better for removing grit and grease because it uses more water, higher temperatures, and longer wash times. The quick 30-minute program is designed for lightly soiled items and may not be sufficient for the heavy residue you are experiencing. Quick, eco and glass programmes are gentler — they often won't shift dried-on food on a full load. Have you been using a quick or eco programme (and will you try a normal or intensive one), or already using a normal / intensive programme?"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "ive been doing the quick 30 min one. ok ill run a normal one now n see"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "What's left on the dishes - sandy grit, greasy film, or white chalky film - and is the machine hot/steamy and audibly spraying during the wash?"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "its just gritty bits on glasses n bowls, mostly top rack. not sure about the spraying tbh, it does get hot tho. also smells a bit musty"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "From what you have described, the most likely cause is spray arms / jets blocked or arm not spinning. Turn each spray arm by hand — it should spin freely — and look at the little jet holes. Most arms unclip so you can rinse them and clear the holes with a cocktail stick. Was an arm stuck or were the jets blocked (and are they clear now), is an arm cracked or broken, or were they all fine?\n[Helpful to see: image \"Check and rinse the spray arm\" — The spray arm spins to fire water through small jet holes. Check it spins freely and rinse the jets clear of food/grit. It usually unclips at the centre. Typical layout — your dishwasher may differ.]"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "ok checked the arms. lower one spins fine but the upper one is stiff. several holes on the upper arm were blocked with seeds n limescale, cleared them with a cocktail stick"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "[Heading: Likely cause: spray arm]\nThe checks point to the spray arm. To find the right part, could you give me the model number? It is usually on the side edge of the door.\n[Panel: \"To check a suitable replacement — Enter your model number below. Once we have the model I can check whether a suitable replacement is available. It's usually on a sticker inside the door or around the frame, or on the back of the appliance.\"]"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "dont know the model number tbh, just know its a beko. do i really need it? i already cleared the arm n filter, will run a normal wash now"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "[Heading: Likely cause: spray arm]\nIt depends on whether the dishwasher is old or has other significant faults. If it is relatively new and working well otherwise, repairing the spray arm issue should be sufficient. The checks point to the spray arm. To find the right part, could you give me the model number? It is usually on the side edge of the door.\n[Panel: \"To check a suitable replacement — Enter your model number below. Once we have the model I can check whether a suitable replacement is available. It's usually on a sticker inside the door or around the frame, or on the back of the appliance.\"]"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "ah ok so its only for buying a new arm? the holes r clear now so i dont think i need one. can i just see how the normal wash goes first n look for the model number if its still gritty?"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "[Heading: Likely cause: spray arm]\nYes, you can run a test cycle to check if cleaning improves before looking for the model number. If grit remains on the glasses and bowls after this wash, then finding the specific model details will be necessary for further advice. The checks point to the spray arm. To find the right part, could you give me the model number? It is usually on the side edge of the door.\n[Panel: \"To check a suitable replacement — Enter your model number below. Once we have the model I can check whether a suitable replacement is available. It's usually on a sticker inside the door or around the frame, or on the back of the appliance.\"]"
  }
 ],
